1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Which database owns this fact? Polyglot persistence: one owner per fact, everything else derived

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:09,000
Same ticket, two screens, two statuses.

3
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:13,000
Both databases answer their own queries. Nothing is down. They still disagree.

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:18,000
Before fixing anything, ask: which copy IS the fact?

5
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:22,000
Each store is chosen for a specific responsibility, not to look advanced.

6
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:24,500
Ownership is per fact. "PostgreSQL is the source of truth" is too broad:

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,900
a filename in PostgreSQL cannot rebuild the attachment bytes.

8
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:31,900
Without an owner, "keep both in sync" is a circular promise.

9
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:38,600
Answer each question once, in writing, for this one fact.

10
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:42,100
Tempting: make the screens agree by copying the resident value back.

11
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:48,100
They agree now, and the stale value has become the "truth". Wrong direction.

12
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:54,600
Repairs flow from the owner to the derived copy, and are recorded.

13
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:58,600
Staff close the ticket. The authoritative value changes first.

14
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:02,600
The projection follows after some lag. If it silently stopped, it would be wrong.

15
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:08,500
The snapshot keeps 'open' on purpose. Old is not the same as wrong.

16
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,500
Every obligation doubles, and new ones appear at the boundary.

17
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,500
Small team, workload fits one model? One database is often the stronger design.

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Use two when the benefit is concrete, ownership is explicit, and you can run both.

19
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:29,600
Remember: Polyglot = different stores for different responsibilities. Each duplicated fact needs ONE authoritative owner. Copies are projections or snapshots: name how and when they refresh. Repairs flow from the owner to the copy, never the reverse. A second database must earn its operating cost.

